## How the CGP examples are built

A CGP project is a template: `CMakeLists.txt`, the library in `cgp/`, your scene in `src/`, the shaders in
`shaders/`. The examples of this tutorial hold only the files that differ from the template. The build script
puts them over a copy of the template, adds the bridge, and builds with the `CMakeLists.txt` of CGP itself:

```
build/<example>-stage/      a copy of the CGP project (CGP_DIR)
  src/                      the template, then the files of the example on top, and the bridge
  CMakeLists.txt            the one of CGP, plus the link options of the bridge
```

This example has no file of its own: it is the template as it is. With the Emscripten SDK activated, from the
root of the repository (or of the unzipped folder):

```
git clone https://github.com/drohmer/cgp-2027.git ../cgp-2027     # the CGP project, once
CGP_DIR=../cgp-2027 python3 scripts/build_examples.py c1-cgp-template
CGP_DIR=../cgp-2027 python3 scripts/build_examples.py --native c1-cgp-template
```

`CGP_DIR` is the folder of the CGP project. *On Windows: see the commands of [Before you start](../01_start/index.html#on-windows).*

The first command builds for the web, copies the result into `examples/c1-cgp-template/web/` and
`examples/c1-cgp-template/lhtml-site/src/_demo/`, then tests it in a browser and captures its poster (when
Playwright is installed, see [Testing a demo](../../03_more/03_tests/index.html)); `--native` also builds the desktop version and runs it for two seconds. In your own CGP project, the
same is three steps: copy `slide_bridge.hpp` and `slide_bridge.cpp` into `src/`, add the link options of the
bridge to the web build of the `CMakeLists.txt`, and use `slide-frame.html` as the page of the demo.

